## 11. Rollback versus redeployment

The framework uses installation, deployment, and rollback scripts. The rollback script may be triggered when installation, initialization, or post-installation tests fail.

### Rollback

A rollback restores a previously working state after an unsuccessful deployment.

Depending on the implementation, it may restore a previous version, reverse changes, or return the installation to its pre-deployment state.

### Redeployment of a previous version

If a user reruns the normal workflow with an older release, this is commonly described as redeploying a previous version.

It can achieve a similar outcome to a rollback, but the mechanism is different.

### Cleanup

If a script only removes temporary or staging files after a failure, it is performing cleanup. Cleanup alone does not restore a previous release.

### Terminology summary

| Operation | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Automatic rollback | Restore a previous or known-good state after failure |
| Redeployment | Deploy an existing release again |
| Redeployment of an older release | Use the normal deployment process with a previous version |
| Cleanup | Remove temporary or unwanted files without necessarily restoring an earlier version |

The exact terminology should reflect what the rollback script actually does.

